(project pricklypear-multipart
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: multipart streaming responses")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section multipart
  (doc "multipart/mixed streaming responses: one HTTP response, many
       independently-targeted htmx swaps. The reactive substrate.")

  (subsection mime-framing
   (doc "One route response streams N MIME parts; each part is one htmx swap")
   (implements image/lib/http_core.ml image/lib/primitives.ml)
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "route returns (multipart (part \"<b>one</b>\") (part \"<i>two</i>\")) => Content-Type: multipart/mixed; both parts present in body")
    (smoke "curl -N shows part one flushed before the handler finishes computing part two")
    (smoke "peer disconnect mid-stream is a non-event (http-robustness invariant 1 holds on this path too)"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Inspired by scriptogre/multipart-response (Python adapters
         for Django/FastAPI/Starlette/FastHTML) and the htmx
         hx-multipart extension. Strip away the framework adapters
         and the idea is small: the server responds
         Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=B and writes parts
         as they are ready. Each part carries its own headers —
         HX-Target / HX-Swap / HX-Part-ID — so every part is an
         independently-targeted htmx swap. One HTTP response becomes
         a stream of targeted DOM updates; no websockets, no SSE
         endpoint, no per-swap round trip.

         NOPALES SURFACE (new prims, VStr-tagged like render-html):
           (part html)                              ; one part, text/html
           (part html :target "#feed" :swap "beforeend" :id "42")
           (json-part json-string :id "result")     ; application/json part
           (multipart p1 p2 ...)                    ; list of parts
           (multipart :target "#feed" :swap "beforeend" p1 p2 ...)
             ; leading kwargs = envelope defaults for every part;
             ; per-part headers override envelope defaults.

         ENVELOPE:
           Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=<random>
           Envelope HX-Target / HX-Swap written as response headers
           (hx-multipart honors them as part defaults).

         PART WIRE FORMAT (RFC 2046, minimal):
           --B\r\nContent-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8\r\n
           HX-Target: #feed\r\nHX-Part-ID: 42\r\n\r\n<body>\r\n
           … final --B--\r\n. Bodies are checked for boundary
           collisions (on collision, regenerate boundary once, then
           raise User_error — do not silently corrupt).

         RENDER PATH:
         respond_handler_value gains a branch: a multipart-tagged
         value sets multipart Content-Type and streams parts through
         the existing Httpun.Body.Writer (write_string + flush per
         part). The closed-writer guard and peer-gone invariants
         from image.http-robustness apply unchanged — a disconnect
         mid-stream MUST NOT take the process down.

         v1 IS EAGER: the handler returns a fully-evaluated list of
         parts; the kernel frames and streams them. You get multiple
         targeted swaps in one response — the reactive payoff —
         without lazy generators in the interpreter. Deferred:
         Nopales-level streaming sources (generator or
         (multipart-stream (lambda (emit) …)) callback style) and
         HX-Last-Part-ID resume for live tails.
   |))
   )
  )

  (subsection hx-multipart-vendored
   (doc "Vendor htmx hx-multipart extension; static asset, no CDN dependency")
   (implements image/lib/http_core.ml)
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "GET /assets/hx-multipart.js returns 200 with the extension source"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         The client half is the hx-multipart htmx extension
         (four.htmx.org/extensions/hx-multipart), which parses the
         multipart stream via fetch-multipart and performs one swap
         per part, honoring per-part HX-Target / HX-Swap / HX-Select
         and the HX-Part-ID / HX-Last-Part-ID reconnect cursor.

         Vendored next to the CodeMirror assets (/assets/cm/*) so the
         image works without a CDN. Pages opt in with hx-ext
         "hx-multipart" on the triggering element.
   |))
   )
  )

  (subsection repl-v2
   (doc "Advanced browser REPL: one input, streamed multipart output — LLM answers, slash commands, live eval")
   (implements libs/repl/lib.pp)
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "type a plain question => LLM answer streams into the transcript as parts arrive")
    (smoke "/note hello there => confirmation part appended; (notes/doc/get …) shows the entry")
    (smoke "(+ 20 22) as input => 42 part appended")
    (smoke "error in eval => error part styled distinctly, transcript intact"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         The advanced REPL is a Nopales package (libs/repl) built on
         the multipart substrate — the dogfood that proves it. One
         interface, one input box, and the response is a multipart
         stream whose parts append to a scrolling transcript.

         INPUT DISPATCH (repl/answer):
           - "/cmd args…"  -> slash command (dispatch table, see below)
           - "(…"          -> Nopales eval via the same Session.eval_one
                              path as browser-repl / /api/eval — NOT a
                              second interpreter
           - otherwise     -> LLM question via libs/ai (ai/ask-shaped:
                              agent-session + context block)

         A single submission can yield SEVERAL parts — that is the
         point. An LLM question streams its deltas as parts (llm_client
         already supports stream:true with on_event callbacks); an
         eval can yield a "running…" part, then the value part, then a
         notes-link part. Each part targets the transcript
         (HX-Target #transcript, HX-Swap beforeend) or replaces a
         placeholder part by id.

         SLASH COMMANDS (v1):
           /note <text>   capture into notes (dailies-and-capture path)
           /eval <expr>   explicit eval (same as bare "(" input)
           /describe <pkg>  package surface summary part
           /branch        current branch + whoami part
           /help          command list part
         Commands are functions in the package; users add their own
         with (repl/command "todo" 'my/handler).

         IDENTITY: runs on the caller's branch with the caller's
         session (route is private by default). LLM key resolution
         follows auth.user-llm-keys (personal key, operator fallback).

         RELATIONSHIP TO EXISTING SURFACES: browser-repl stays — the
         simple /repl + POST /api/eval path is the low-level tool
         (and the agent API). repl-v2 is the human cockpit: LLM +
         commands + eval in one streamed transcript. If it earns its
         place, /repl can later point at it; that decision is NOT
         part of this section.
   |))
   )
  )
 )
)
